WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:04.704
-What happens now moving forward
in terms of, I mean,

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
first and foremost,
these separated families?

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.309 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you have any optimism that
those 2,000 children

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:11.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
are gonna be reunited with
their parents?

00:00:11.144 --> 00:00:14.047 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I hold out optimism,
if only because I think that

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:16.082 align:start size:94% position:6%
the public pressure
has had some effect.

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:19.252
Perversely, as you mentioned at
the top of the show,

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:22.188
there's now this, kind of,
choice that's being offered,

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:24.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is, like,
you can get your kid back

00:00:24.424 --> 00:00:25.225 line:92%
if you agree to be deported

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:27.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you renounce all your --
your claims to asylum.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I think a lot of people
are gonna be deport--

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.097 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
reunited that way.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
But it's going to take
sustained public pressure.

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:36.136
I mean, what's very clear is,
we broke a story a year ago

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:38.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
when they first started
thinking about this policy.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:39.339 line:92%
And they thought about it

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:40.707 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they crafted
internal procedures about it.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:42.642
And someone leaked us documents,
'cause there was this --

00:00:42.642 --> 00:00:44.978
-This is the separation policy.
-Child separation policy.

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:45.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
People were so freaked out
about it

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.915
and they cogitated about it for
a year, and they implemented it.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:51.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
They had no plan to reunite
these families.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:54.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
They had no plan,
because they didn't care.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.422 line:92%
I mean, that's what, I think,

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:57.257 align:start size:94% position:6%
is so shocking
about this whole thing.

00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:00.527
-How did it go from -- I mean,
so, 2017 you break this story.

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:01.761 line:92%
Obviously, it takes, you know,

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:03.196 align:start size:94% position:6%
up to near a year
to come through.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:05.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
Were there people
in the administration

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:06.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
who knew it would play
like this?

00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:09.602
-I think so. And I think the
reason it didn't happen was,

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:11.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
precisely, because
the experts who --

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.806 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
there are people who work in

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:14.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Department
of Homeland Security

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.142 align:start size:94% position:6%
and in the asylum office
and things like this

00:01:16.142 --> 00:01:18.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
who understand
that you can't do this.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:20.413 size:88% position:44%
And I think they warned
against it successfully.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think the President in a fit
of pique, recently,

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:25.852
has been ramped up about how,
you know, stressed out he is

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:26.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the border.
-Yeah.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:29.189 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And the "invasion"
and the "infestation"

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:29.889 size:81% position:41% line:92%
that's coming over it,

00:01:29.889 --> 00:01:31.791
and I think he managed to
prevail on his advisers

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:34.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
against a lot of the better
judgment of the civil servants

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:36.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
in those agencies
to start doing this.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, then he signs
an executive order, you know,

00:01:39.099 --> 00:01:39.766 size:81% position:41% line:92%
which ostensibly says,

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:41.434
"We're gonna get these families
back together."

00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:43.870
He says, you know, on
television, he just couldn't --

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.039 size:88% position:44%
it was too awful to have
families be separated.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.041
What do you think happened there
for him

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.275 line:92%
to ultimately reverse himself?

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.977 align:start size:94% position:6%
Although we are hearing
whispers now

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:53.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he's saying he wishes
he hadn't done that.

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:55.949 size:84% position:42%
-I think the President
sits in the White House

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.650 line:92%
and watches cable news all day.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.252 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-Yeah. Good for you.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.753 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:01.421 size:75% position:38% line:92%
-Good for us, maybe.

00:02:01.421 --> 00:02:02.822 align:start size:94% position:6%
Bad for the country probably.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:05.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
Probably not a -- probably
not a ton of MSNBC.

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:06.926 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Although, yes.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:08.561
Although he -- more than I think
he would ever admit.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:10.396 size:81% position:41%
-Right, right. Yeah.
-You know what I mean?

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, there's times
where it's very clear --

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.501 line:92%
he does a lot of hate-watching.

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:14.934 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Which, again, if you're
hate-watching, cool.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:17.904 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:17.904 --> 00:02:19.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, whatever --
I don't judge.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:22.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
-They all have to
watch the commercials.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:24.277 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-We make no discernment.

00:02:24.277 --> 00:02:26.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, I mean, basically, I think
he's sitting there

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.314
and he's watching himself
take a beating on this

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.850
in public opinion and feels like
he has to walk it back.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.752 size:75% position:38%
And I thought it was
a significant moment

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:33.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the history of
this administration

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.823
because we've seen him penned in
before by the courts, you know,

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:38.825
when they came in against
the travel ban or DACA.

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:41.494
We've seen him penned in by
Congress when John McCain

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.328 line:92%
gave the thumbs-down for ACA.

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.764
This was really the first time
that I felt like civil society,

00:02:44.764 --> 00:02:48.101
you know, in -- in a broad
sense, basically, kind of,

00:02:48.101 --> 00:02:50.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
stood up and said,
"No, you can't do this."

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.772 line:92%
-You mentioned civil society.

00:02:52.772 --> 00:02:53.907 line:92%
I will segue to civility.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:56.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
That seems to be
the conversation today.

00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's a tricky thing to talk
about, because I think we all,

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:02.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, we all would expect
to be treated civilly.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:03.550 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Like, we all want that.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:07.487
How do you feel about right now
Republicans saying

00:03:07.487 --> 00:03:09.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there is now
a lack of civility?

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.324 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, look, I under--
I want to, like, acknowledge

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:13.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
what you're saying, which is
I get people's impulse.

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:15.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't think it's a bad
impulse to have of, like,

00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:17.797
"Yeah, don't yell at people."
Or, be, you know -- I get that.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's a human and
understandable part of

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.367 align:start size:94% position:6%
people's impulses
around this stuff.

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:24.504
And, generally, I think that's
a good guiding post for people.

00:03:24.504 --> 00:03:27.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
I will say this that,
look, Sarah Huckabee Sanders,

00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:31.244
for example, is one of the most
powerful people in the world.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:34.948
And in a very real and tangible
sense the defining feature of

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:36.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
a free society
is that you can tell

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:40.587
one of the most powerful people
in the world in your government,

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.422 line:92%
"Get out of my restaurant."

00:03:42.422 --> 00:03:42.889 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Right.

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:45.959
-Like, that's genuinely, like, a
thing that makes a free society.

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:46.492 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Applause ]

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:50.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, now, people could say,
like, that --

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:52.999
I don't think they should
do that or that's rude.

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.668 size:88% position:44%
And I totally get people
on either side of that.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:56.936 line:92%
But in a real deep sense, like,

00:03:56.936 --> 00:03:58.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
hectoring, yelling at
public officials?

00:03:58.638 --> 00:04:01.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I covered all the
Tea Party town halls of 2009.

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:04.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was a festival of yelling
at public officials.

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:05.111 size:75% position:38% line:92%
And in the same way,

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:06.980
that's actually
an important right to preserve.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think sometimes that this
conversation about civility

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:14.187 size:88% position:44%
can paper over a lot of
what is actually just --

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:14.887 line:92%
what politics looks like.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:17.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And, you know, I feel, like,
lost in this, obviously,

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
Sarah Huckabee Sanders,
in her tweet, framed her --

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.829
she focused on her own
politeness in politely leaving.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:26.699
It does seem as though she was
also politely asked to leave.

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:27.166 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes.

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.601 size:81% position:41%
-Now, again, you know,
at the end of the day,

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.536
I think if you're asked to leave
a restaurant

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:34.140
you don't look back and go,
"They were so nice about it."

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:34.674 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:36.943
-They did -- They did comp them
the cheese plate.

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.479 line:92%
-Yes. That's no small thing.

00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:40.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:45.585
But, you know, and it did strike
me as interesting that the woman

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:48.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
who owned the restaurant,
she went and asked her staff.

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
She made it very clear that
she had, you know,

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:54.360
she had an ethnic staff, she had
a staff that were, you know --

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.729 size:84% position:42%
gay people on her staff
that made it difficult.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:59.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she actually asked them
their opinion, which --

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:04.437
-I thought it was, look, again,
I feel like the basic --

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.507 line:92%
my basic principles here are --

00:05:07.507 --> 00:05:11.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
nonviolent action
in dissent or in protest,

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:12.979 line:92%
sort of across viewpoints,

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:16.382 size:88% position:44%
is part of what American
political life is about.

00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:19.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, that's --
[ Applause ]

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:22.655 line:92%
That's like -- and I think --

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:26.926
And I think people feeling moral
urgency about a 12-month-old

00:05:26.926 --> 00:05:31.397
who is sitting in some facility
a thousand miles away

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
from her mother,
who's been torn away from her,

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:37.370
I think feeling some sense that,
like, this is a crisis.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a moral crisis
that requires me

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:41.507 align:start size:94% position:6%
to take
some extra action, again,

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:45.011
within the sort of confines of,
you know, what's protected

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:48.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
under law and our democratic,
sort of, principles.

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.650 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that
that makes a lot of sense.

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:51.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's always so great
to have you here.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much
for making the time for us.

00:05:53.453 --> 00:05:55.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you, man.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:56.889 size:84% position:42% line:92%
Chris Hayes, everybody.

